dict(section="Assignment 2", title="Part C · Swin by hand (d = 5: 448×448, C = 128)",
     bullets=["Embedding: 448/4 = 112 → 112×112×128 (stage 1 same)",
              "Stages: 112²×128 → 56²×256 → 28²×512 → 14²×1024",
              "Windows (7×7): 16² = 256 → 8² = 64 → 4² = 16 → 2² = 4",
              "hw = 12,544, C² = 16,384:  4hwC² = 822,083,584",
              "Ω(MSA) = 822,083,584 + 2(hw)²C 40,282,095,616 = 41,104,179,200",
              "Ω(W-MSA) = 822,083,584 + 2M²hwC 157,351,936 = 979,435,520",
              "Ratio = (2C + hw)/(2C + M²) = 12,800/305 ≈ 41.97;  attention-only ratio hw/M² = 256"],
     narration="Part C, for d equals 5. Patch size 4 on a 448 image gives 112, so the linear embedding is 112 by 112 by 128, and stage 1 keeps that. Each later stage halves height and width and doubles channels: 56 by 56 by 256, 28 by 28 by 512, and 14 by 14 by 1024. Windows are 7 by 7, so the counts are 112 over 7 squared, 256; then 64, 16 and 4. For the cost: h w is 112 squared, 12,544. The projection term 4 h w C squared is 822 million and is the same in both formulas. Global attention adds 2 h w squared C, about 40.3 billion, total about 41.1 billion. Window attention adds 2 M squared h w C, about 157 million, total about 979 million. The ratio is about 42. A neat check: divide both by 2 h w C and the ratio becomes 2C plus h w over 2C plus M squared, 12,800 over 305. If you compare only the attention terms you get h w over M squared, 256, which is exactly the number of stage-1 windows; the shared projection term is why the full ratio is smaller."),

