dict(section="Viva drill", title="Rapid-fire: answers you should say in one breath",
     bullets=["Why predict ε, not x0? Ho et al.'s simplified ELBO; stable unit-variance target",
              "Why √ᾱ closed form? Gaussian steps compose: q(x_t|x0) = N(√ᾱ x0, (1−ᾱ) I)",
              "Why no noise at the last step? output is the mean estimate of x0",
              "Why 197 tokens? 196 patches + [CLS] · Why Swin linear cost? M²·hw vs (hw)²",
              "LoRA: frozen W + low-rank BA; B = 0 at start · QLoRA: NF4 base + LoRA + fp16 compute",
              "IA3: learned scaling vectors on K, V, FFN · macro-F1: unweighted mean of per-class F1",
              "Selection on validation, report test · mean ± SD over the two seeds"],
     narration="Let's finish with a rapid-fire round. Why predict the noise instead of x zero? It is Ho et al.'s simplified training objective, a reweighted variational bound, and noise is a stable unit-variance target. Why can we jump to any t? Because Gaussian steps compose, so x t given x zero is a Gaussian with mean root alpha bar x zero and variance one minus alpha bar. Why no noise at the last step? Because the output should be the mean estimate of the clean image. Why 197 tokens in ViT? 196 patches plus the CLS token. Why is Swin's attention linear in image size? Because each token only attends inside its 7 by 7 window, so the cost is M squared times h w instead of h w squared. LoRA: a frozen weight plus a low-rank product B A, with B starting at zero. QLoRA: the same, but on a 4-bit NF4 base with float 16 compute. IA3: learned vectors that rescale keys, values and feed-forward activations. Macro F1: the unweighted mean of per-class F1, which is why one ignored class pulls it down. And always: choose on validation, report on test, mean plus or minus standard deviation over the two seeds."),

